{
  "concept": "Enzyme specificity can be explained by two models: a rigid pre-shaped active site (lock-and-key) or an active site that changes conformation as the substrate binds (induced fit).",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Panel (a) heading: '(a) Lock-and-key model'",
    "Panel (b) heading: '(b) Induced fit model'",
    "Enzyme body: large rounded dome/trapezoid shape, pale yellow-green fill with thick olive-green outline, labeled 'Enzyme' beneath",
    "Active site: notched cleft in the top surface of the enzyme, shaped as two small pockets separated by a ridge",
    "Two substrate molecules: lavender/purple rounded shapes with dark outlines — one rectangular-lobed, one round-lobed — each with a small tan/peach knob on its lower binding face",
    "Label 'Substrates' with two thin leader lines pointing to each purple substrate",
    "Annotation inside panel (a) left diagram: 'Active site is proper shape' with two curved arrows pointing down into the cleft",
    "Annotation inside panel (b) left diagram: 'Active site changes to fit' with two curved arrows pointing down into the cleft",
    "Caption over bound state in both panels: 'Substrate complex formed'",
    "Black vertical contact line between the two seated substrates in the bound state (substrate–substrate interface)",
    "Black thickened contact segments where substrate knobs meet the active-site surface in the bound state",
    "Four-diagram layout: unbound (left) and bound (right) within each of two side-by-side panels",
    "Optional comparison overlay: dashed grey outline of the unbound active-site contour retained on the bound enzyme to reveal (or rule out) shape change"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "modelToggle",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Both models explain specificity, but only induced fit requires the enzyme itself to change shape when the substrate arrives.",
      "label": "Binding model",
      "options": ["Lock-and-key", "Induced fit"],
      "default": "Lock-and-key"
    },
    {
      "id": "bindingProgress",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "By sweeping the substrate from free to fully docked, the learner sees that in lock-and-key the active-site geometry is identical at every step, while in induced fit the pocket progressively molds around the substrate.",
      "label": "Substrate docking progress",
      "range": [0, 1, 0.01],
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "shapeGhost",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Overlaying the original unbound active-site outline makes the conformational change of induced fit measurable — and its absence in lock-and-key obvious.",
      "label": "Show unbound active-site outline",
      "default": false
    },
    {
      "id": "playBinding",
      "type": "animation",
      "teaches": "Watching the docking run in real time links substrate approach to the enzyme's conformational response as a single continuous event.",
      "label": "Play substrate binding",
      "loop": true
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The enzyme and its active site",
      "narration": "A large green enzyme carries a notched cleft on its surface — the active site. Two purple substrate molecules float above it, unbound.",
      "state": { "modelToggle": "Lock-and-key", "bindingProgress": 0, "shapeGhost": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Lock-and-key: a pre-shaped pocket",
      "narration": "Drag the substrates down. The cleft already matches their contours exactly, like a key entering a lock — the enzyme never alters its shape.",
      "state": { "modelToggle": "Lock-and-key", "bindingProgress": 0.5, "shapeGhost": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Lock-and-key complex formed",
      "narration": "The substrates seat snugly and the enzyme–substrate complex forms. The dashed outline of the original active site lies exactly on the bound one: zero conformational change.",
      "state": { "modelToggle": "Lock-and-key", "bindingProgress": 1, "shapeGhost": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Induced fit: an open, flexible site",
      "narration": "Switch models. Before binding, this active site is wider and less complementary — it is not yet the right shape.",
      "state": { "modelToggle": "Induced fit", "bindingProgress": 0, "shapeGhost": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "The site reshapes as the substrate approaches",
      "narration": "As the substrates descend, the enzyme's surface bends inward to wrap around them. Specificity is created during binding, not before it.",
      "state": { "modelToggle": "Induced fit", "bindingProgress": 0.5, "shapeGhost": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Induced-fit complex formed",
      "narration": "The complex is formed with a tight, molded contact surface. The dashed original outline now clearly differs from the bound shape — the substrate induced the fit.",
      "state": { "modelToggle": "Induced fit", "bindingProgress": 1, "shapeGhost": true }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Both models give the same end result — a tightly matched enzyme–substrate complex — but lock-and-key assumes the match exists beforehand, while induced fit says the substrate itself sculpts the active site into its complementary shape.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two side-by-side panels sharing a common style. Enzyme: broad rounded dome with flat-ish bottom, pale yellow-green fill, heavy olive-green stroke, word 'Enzyme' centered below. Active site is a small double-notch cleft at the top center with a central ridge. Substrates: two lavender shapes with black outlines — left one squarish with a downward stem, right one circular with a stepped notch — each ending in a small tan/peach binding knob; label 'Substrates' above with thin leader lines. In the unbound frame show curved black arrows from the annotation text down into the cleft, with text 'Active site is proper shape' (lock-and-key) or 'Active site changes to fit' (induced fit). In the bound frame, substrates sit in the cleft, a bold black vertical line marks the substrate–substrate interface and short bold black segments mark the substrate–enzyme contacts, with 'Substrate complex formed' above. Panel captions '(a) Lock-and-key model' and '(b) Induced fit model' centered beneath. When the model toggle is set to induced fit, the unbound cleft should be visibly wider and shallower than the bound cleft; in lock-and-key the cleft geometry must be pixel-identical between unbound and bound states. The ghost overlay is a grey dashed copy of the unbound active-site contour drawn over the bound enzyme. Interpolate substrate position and (for induced fit only) the cleft control points smoothly with the docking slider."
}

