userContent.text:
Build an interactive figure using SVG.js.

CONCEPT: Enzyme specificity can be explained by two models: a rigid pre-shaped active site (lock-and-key) or an active site that changes conformation as the substrate binds (induced fit).
KEY INSIGHT (what students should walk away understanding): Both models give the same end result — a tightly matched enzyme–substrate complex — but lock-and-key assumes the match exists beforehand, while induced fit says the substrate itself sculpts the active site into its complementary shape.
DEMO STEPS: 6 guided steps — implement the step player in #ui

FULL BLUEPRINT:
{
  "concept": "Enzyme specificity can be explained by two models: a rigid pre-shaped active site (lock-and-key) or an active site that changes conformation as the substrate binds (induced fit).",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Panel (a) heading: '(a) Lock-and-key model'",
    "Panel (b) heading: '(b) Induced fit model'",
    "Enzyme body: large rounded dome/trapezoid shape, pale yellow-green fill with thick olive-green outline, labeled 'Enzyme' beneath",
    "Active site: notched cleft in the top surface of the enzyme, shaped as two small pockets separated by a ridge",
    "Two substrate molecules: lavender/purple rounded shapes with dark outlines — one rectangular-lobed, one round-lobed — each with a small tan/peach knob on its lower binding face",
    "Label 'Substrates' with two thin leader lines pointing to each purple substrate",
    "Annotation inside panel (a) left diagram: 'Active site is proper shape' with two curved arrows pointing down into the cleft",
    "Annotation inside panel (b) left diagram: 'Active site changes to fit' with two curved arrows pointing down into the cleft",
    "Caption over bound state in both panels: 'Substrate complex formed'",
    "Black vertical contact line between the two seated substrates in the bound state (substrate–substrate interface)",
    "Black thickened contact segments where substrate knobs meet the active-site surface in the bound state",
    "Four-diagram layout: unbound (left) and bound (right) within each of two side-by-side panels",
    "Optional comparison overlay: dashed grey outline of the unbound active-site contour retained on the bound enzyme to reveal (or rule out) shape change"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "modelToggle",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Both models explain specificity, but only induced fit requires the enzyme itself to change shape when the substrate arrives.",
      "label": "Binding model",
      "options": [
        "Lock-and-key",
        "Induced fit"
